meta {
  name: The shape of the day and the plan of the week
  seq: 12
}

docs {
  Four things have to exist before a class can be said to happen, and they are
  built here in that order: rooms, a period scheme with periods in it, bell
  schedules that give those periods clock times, and a calendar of days saying
  which dates are in session. Only then does a timetable of weekly slots mean
  anything.

  Two facts govern everything below and neither is guessable from the routes.

  Times are integer minutes from midnight and they live only on
  bell_schedule_periods: 545 is 09:05. A period carries a name, a code and a
  sequence and no time at all, because the same Period 1 rings at a different
  hour in winter and the bell schedule is what differs. Nothing here accepts a
  clock string or an ISO timestamp for a period boundary.

  A date is in session if and only if a calendar_days row exists for that
  branch and date and the relevant half of it is open. No row means not in
  session - not "probably a normal Tuesday". A holiday is a row with both
  halves false, which is a different thing from no row at all and reads
  differently in the calendar. Requests 44 to 47 show all three answers.

  There is a third rule the code enforces and no route name reveals: a
  calendar day that is in session but has no calendar_day_schedules row
  produces no classes whatsoever. Occurrences are resolved per day schedule,
  so a day with bells attached has a timetable and a day without one is open
  but empty. Requests 37 to 39 attach them, and folder 13 depends on it - skip
  them and every register refuses with attendance_not_scheduled rather than
  the refusal you were testing for.

  Nothing here is deleted in this folder. Folder 13 tears down the whole
  scaffolding once it has finished marking attendance against it, because
  every row below is a precondition of something there.
}
